// The cdz-kernel REDUCER world — the guest boundary of the v2 agent-harness kernel, as a wasm
// component-model interface (design/agent-harness-kernel.md §19b). The operator's steer: model the
// kernel's core boundary as the component model FROM THE START, not a Rust trait later mapped — so
// this .wit IS the contract, and the wasmtime component host binds to it. (The Rust `Reducer` trait
// in src/reducer.rs is the INTERIM until this world + the wasmtime host land; it mirrors this shape.)
//
// ── The fold, as a component ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// A reducer is a pure fold: given the event being applied, it returns the effects it wants performed
// (§3, §16c-S4). It holds NO state across calls — everything it needs to continue lives in the KV
// store, which it reads/writes via the `kv` HOST IMPORT (a DIRECT read, NOT an effect — §4/§4b: a
// reducer querying its own KV is not a world-touching effect). Effects are REQUESTS (§5): the guest
// returns them; the HOST authorizes (SEC-F1), durably dispatches (§16c-S1), executes, and folds the
// result back as a later event the guest will be called with again.
//
// ── Payloads are OPAQUE bytes at this boundary ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Event/effect payloads are `list<u8>` here — the kernel treats them as opaque (design principle:
// "kernel sees hashes + opaque bytes; what's behind them is Cadenza's business"). The CONTENTS are
// Cadenza binary-sexpr (§19a — the shared cadenza-syntax::codec bottom crate), but that's a
// guest/host userspace agreement ABOVE this wire, NOT something the component boundary decodes.
// Large payloads are content-addressed blobs (a hash in the bytes); small ones inline — the guest
// decides, the boundary just carries bytes. Keeping payloads opaque here is what keeps this world
// codec-independent (so it can land BEFORE the §19a extraction).
//
// ── Determinism (§3, §16c-S3) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// The guest MUST be a pure function of (event, kv). It may not read the clock, network, or entropy
// directly — those enter only as effects whose results arrive as later `apply` calls. The wasm
// sandbox enforces the absence of ambient I/O; this contract documents the intent. KV reads during a
// fold are point-in-time (the host serves KV as-of the folds before this event).
//
// ⚠ CONTRACT STABILITY: like cadenza:runtime, the SHAPE below is the host↔guest contract. Additive
// evolution only (new effect kinds appended, new optional fields) — never reorder/remove. This is a
// DRAFT for review (v-syntax/rcdzc are not consumers; the wasmtime host is the next slice) — the
// world is not yet wired to a host, so it can still be reshaped before the first component binds it.
package cadenza:agent-kernel;

// ── Shared value types: NONE at this boundary anymore (bytes-AST fold, DESIGN-binary-ast-abi §2/§3a) ──
// The former `types` interface (content-type, effect-kind, effect-request) is GONE. Under the bytes-AST
// fold boundary the event and the returned effect list each cross as ONE `list<u8>` cadenza-ast value-form
// document, so content-type/effect-kind/effect-request are no longer WIT records — they are value-form AST
// SHAPES the guest and kernel agree on ABOVE the wire (the kernel builds/parses them via
// `ast_marshal.rs`; the guest via `cadenza-ast`). This is the "full collapse, delete the old thing"
// (no-adapter directive): the boundary carries a self-describing binary AST, not a hand-split WIT tuple
// whose fields the host must know — a new envelope/effect field is a new named AST child, never a WIT
// signature change (additive-by-symbol). The effect KIND rides as a family STRING inside the AST (never a
// closed enum discriminant), so a register-by-string userspace family with no built-in kind round-trips.

// ── The KV store, as a HOST IMPORT the guest calls directly ─────────────────────────────────────────
// Per §4/§4b, a reducer reading its OWN KV is a direct query, NOT a world-touching effect — so KV is a
// host-provided IMPORT interface the guest calls inline during a fold, not something it `apply`s an
// effect for. (Log + KV stay HOST concerns — §19b: traits on the host side; this is their guest-facing
// import surface.) Keys/values are opaque bytes (the guest defines their schema — §4).
interface kv {
  get: func(key: list<u8>) -> option<list<u8>>;
  put: func(key: list<u8>, value: list<u8>);
  delete: func(key: list<u8>) -> bool;
  // Keys with `prefix`, in canonical (byte-ascending) order — deterministic (§16c-S8) so a fold over
  // a scan emits effects in a replay-stable order.
  prefix-scan: func(prefix: list<u8>) -> list<tuple<list<u8>, list<u8>>>;
}

// ── The fold export — the guest's one entrypoint ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
interface fold {
  // Fold ONE event into the KV, returning the effects requested (§3). Called once per event, in log
  // order. Both directions are ONE cadenza-ast value-form document as opaque bytes (DESIGN-binary-ast-abi
  // §3a — the kernel host never constructs or inspects a value-heap handle):
  //
  //   IN  `event`: the whole event as one AST document folding the former three args into named children,
  //        e.g. `(record (= content-type <ct>) (= payload <bytes-or-absent>) (= resumes <bytes-or-absent>)
  //        (= outcome <outcome-or-absent>))`.
  //        `content-type` is the routing/filtering hint; `payload` is the opaque request body; `resumes`
  //        is — for a result/timer event — the guest's OWN continuation token, echoed verbatim from the
  //        originating effect-request's `correlation` (the single resume mechanism), absent for inbound
  //        messages / genesis / denials / fire-and-forget. `outcome` is the DISCRIMINATED effect-result view
  //        `option<Ok(list<u8>) | Err{message:list<u8>, retryable:bool} | TimedOut>` — present (`Some`) ONLY
  //        on an effect-result event so the guest can tell a successful reply from a failure (the raw
  //        `payload` bytes drop that discriminant), `None` for every other event kind. The kernel builds this
  //        document (`ast_marshal::build_event_document`); the guest decodes it.
  //   OUT `list<u8>`: the requested effects as one AST document — a value-form list of effect-request
  //        records `(correlation? kind:string payload? target:bytes)`. The kernel parses it
  //        (`ast_marshal::parse_effect_list`). `kind` is the family STRING (never a closed enum), so a
  //        register-by-string userspace family with no built-in kind round-trips.
  //
  // Any guest — Cadenza OR Rust — that can decode/encode `cadenza-ast` bytes is a valid reducer; there is
  // no value-heap handle ABI. Genesis/setup context arrive as ordinary early `apply` calls, so there is no
  // separate init export.
  //
  // TOTALITY (§17 "can't-brick"): the guest must not trap for any input — an event it doesn't
  // understand should yield an empty effect list (an empty `(effects)` document), not a crash. (The host
  // treats a guest trap as a fold failure; the trap→event policy is a host concern, §16c gap A.)
  apply: func(event: list<u8>) -> list<u8>;
}

// ── The reducer world: what a reducer component EXPORTS + IMPORTS ────────────────────────────────────
world reducer {
  // The guest reads/writes its session KV directly (a non-effect host import — §4b).
  import kv;
  // The guest exports the fold entrypoint the host drives per event.
  export fold;
}
